WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.190 --> 00:00:12.490
There’s a question in theoretical physics
that you may never have considered, but unlike

00:00:12.490 --> 00:00:18.270
many other hot science questions, this is
one that hardly any physicist will answer

00:00:18.270 --> 00:00:20.200
‘no’ to.

00:00:20.200 --> 00:00:21.200
The question?

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:26.320
‘Is there more to the universe than what
we’re able to see?’

00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:32.230
The observable universe is a sphere (we think),
with planet Earth at its centre – it encompasses

00:00:32.230 --> 00:00:36.809
the limits of what we are able to observe
with current technology – that’s around

00:00:36.809 --> 00:00:40.690
46.5 billion light-years in all directions.

00:00:40.690 --> 00:00:46.679
The idea that our universe stops there, that
if we were to hop into SS Superluminal and

00:00:46.679 --> 00:00:54.690
travel 46.5 billion light-years in one direction
all we’d find is an impassable loading screen,

00:00:54.690 --> 00:01:01.910
is, well… it goes far beyond egocentricity
and into outright lunacy - with a little dash

00:01:01.910 --> 00:01:06.130
of geocentricity thrown in for good measure,
of course.

00:01:06.130 --> 00:01:12.370
So, if you were to ask any theoretical physicist
or astronomer ‘is the observable universe

00:01:12.370 --> 00:01:14.330
all there is?’

00:01:14.330 --> 00:01:19.470
Not one would answer ‘yes’, and if they
did, there’s a good chance you’ve taken

00:01:19.470 --> 00:01:25.690
a wrong turn and are no longer in the University,
but have gotten lost in the local Aldi, down

00:01:25.690 --> 00:01:27.180
the cheese aisle.

00:01:27.180 --> 00:01:33.460
No, the cardigan-fueled arguments only arise
when you ask professors a slightly different

00:01:33.460 --> 00:01:39.570
question - not, ‘is there more,’ but ‘how
much more is there?’.

00:01:39.570 --> 00:01:44.060
If the answer you receive is ‘well we’ve
got another ten packs of double Gloucester

00:01:44.060 --> 00:01:49.160
in the storeroom’, then seriously, you need
to leave the guy at the deli counter alone.

00:01:49.160 --> 00:01:51.390
He doesn’t know shit about the universe.

00:01:51.390 --> 00:01:55.590
‘How much more is there?’ is currently
one of the most hotly debated questions on

00:01:55.590 --> 00:02:00.920
the science scene, right after ‘how can
this triple-stack sandwich be so delicious

00:02:00.920 --> 00:02:03.500
when it doesn’t obey Occam's razor?’.

00:02:03.500 --> 00:02:09.099
MIT theoretical physicist Alan Guth is one
of the most influential minds in the field

00:02:09.099 --> 00:02:11.120
- the universe, that is.

00:02:11.120 --> 00:02:12.160
Not sandwiches.

00:02:12.160 --> 00:02:17.670
He estimates the size of the unobservable
universe, all that is beyond our field of

00:02:17.670 --> 00:02:25.600
view, to be 3 × 1023 times the radius of
the observable universe.

00:02:25.600 --> 00:02:30.440
Now, Alan Guth is, it’s fair to say, a rather
smart cookie.

00:02:30.440 --> 00:02:35.560
He doesn’t just enjoy spending his time
making our brains hurt by peppering them with

00:02:35.560 --> 00:02:39.240
large numbers, he wants to liquify them entirely.

00:02:39.240 --> 00:02:46.000
You see, Guth is one of the leading proponents
of the idea that our universe may be one of

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:49.090
many within a larger multiverse.

00:02:49.090 --> 00:02:54.240
But Guth was far from the first to play around
with this idea.

00:02:54.240 --> 00:03:00.720
The Ancient Greeks no less, postulated in
the 5th century BC that all matter is composed

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:08.870
of unseen particles called atoms, from the
Greek ‘atomos’ meaning ‘indivisible’.

00:03:08.870 --> 00:03:13.070
Over the following centuries philosophers
such as Chrysippus would go on to suggest

00:03:13.070 --> 00:03:18.980
that when atoms collide they create an infinite
number of multiple universes that eventually

00:03:18.980 --> 00:03:23.360
expire and regenerate in an eternal cycle.

00:03:23.360 --> 00:03:24.360
Deep stuff.

00:03:24.360 --> 00:03:30.520
But there wasn’t a great lot those peplos-wearing
polymaths could do with this mind-bending

00:03:30.520 --> 00:03:31.520
information.

00:03:31.520 --> 00:03:36.531
They couldn’t whip out their Olympus electron
microscopes – not that Olympus – and zoom

00:03:36.531 --> 00:03:41.430
in to the atomic level to disprove their hypothesis.

00:03:41.430 --> 00:03:46.700
Only now, more than two millennia on, can
we measure all the weird invisible bits of

00:03:46.700 --> 00:03:48.349
our world.

00:03:48.349 --> 00:03:52.160
Things like cosmic background radiation and
gravity.

00:03:52.160 --> 00:03:54.090
And what have we discovered?

00:03:54.090 --> 00:04:00.599
We inhabit an expanding universe that, like
an unplanned child, was banged into existence

00:04:00.599 --> 00:04:04.110
some 13.8 billion years ago.

00:04:04.110 --> 00:04:12.540
But to take this disturbing analogy a little
further - who, or more likely what, was our

00:04:12.540 --> 00:04:13.540
parent?

00:04:13.540 --> 00:04:19.870
The most obvious way to answer this question
is to ask what happened during, and even just

00:04:19.870 --> 00:04:21.709
before the big bang.

00:04:21.709 --> 00:04:28.540
But it turns out attempting to figure out
what existed before anything existed, when

00:04:28.540 --> 00:04:35.470
that thing is your entire existence, is well,
about as easy as it sounds – the last time

00:04:35.470 --> 00:04:39.330
we tried we ended up with God, which answered
absolutely nothing.

00:04:39.330 --> 00:04:45.480
But that didn’t stop Alan Guth from having
a stab at it, along with his colleagues Andrei

00:04:45.480 --> 00:04:49.930
Linde, Paul Steinhardt, and Andreas Albrecht.

00:04:49.930 --> 00:04:55.410
In the eighties they came up with Inflation
Theory, which combines ideas from quantum

00:04:55.410 --> 00:05:01.820
physics and particle physics to posit that
just prior to the big bang there was a period

00:05:01.820 --> 00:05:06.900
of faster-than-the-speed-of-light inflation
- funnily enough the same thing happened in

00:05:06.900 --> 00:05:09.100
Germany after the first world war.

00:05:09.100 --> 00:05:13.374
During these initial moments – less than
a millisecond after the big bang – the universe

00:05:13.374 --> 00:05:19.490
went from the size of a billionth of a proton,
to roughly the size of an orange.

00:05:19.490 --> 00:05:24.130
After which, traditional big bang expansion
took over, in a period sometimes referred

00:05:24.130 --> 00:05:27.050
to as the ‘hot big bang’.

00:05:27.050 --> 00:05:31.090
Inflation theory neatly answers the consistency
conundrum.

00:05:31.090 --> 00:05:35.210
You see, our universe is remarkably uniform.

00:05:35.210 --> 00:05:41.130
Up close it looks like a morning after an
interstellar barn dance, on acid with a galaxy

00:05:41.130 --> 00:05:46.199
here, a black hole minding its own business
over there, and a nebula pissing about somewhere

00:05:46.199 --> 00:05:48.050
in the background.

00:05:48.050 --> 00:05:53.840
But if you were to zoom out - and I mean really
zoom out - like Hubble Ultra Deep Field and

00:05:53.840 --> 00:06:01.530
then some zoomed out, you would begin to notice
a pattern – or more accurately, a lack of

00:06:01.530 --> 00:06:02.940
one.

00:06:02.940 --> 00:06:09.150
Space basically all looks the same, with the
googleplex of assorted items chilling out

00:06:09.150 --> 00:06:17.020
in our universe being very, very evenly distributed
- like grains of sand on a perfectly flat

00:06:17.020 --> 00:06:18.639
beach.

00:06:18.639 --> 00:06:24.470
In fact, if you measure the intensity of the
background radiation in any two randomly sampled

00:06:24.470 --> 00:06:30.720
patches of the universe, they will come out
pretty much exactly the same, with a maximum

00:06:30.720 --> 00:06:32.550
variation of 0.001%.

00:06:32.550 --> 00:06:43.000
If the big bang just happened, with no pre-expansion
period, this homogeneity wouldn’t be possible.

00:06:43.000 --> 00:06:48.710
It’s difficult to know exactly what a non-inflation
universe would look like, but according to

00:06:48.710 --> 00:06:54.910
some models we would see a super-concentrated
mass of galaxies surrounded by vast areas

00:06:54.910 --> 00:06:59.500
of nothing interspersed with the odd bit of
matter.

00:06:59.500 --> 00:07:05.170
Inflation allowed the universe to achieve
uniformity before it ‘banged’, which wasn’t

00:07:05.170 --> 00:07:11.040
a bang per se, but a slightly slower expansion
of space over billions of years.

00:07:11.040 --> 00:07:16.199
Think of it like thoroughly mixing your batter
before baking a cake instead of just tossing

00:07:16.199 --> 00:07:21.160
a load of flour and eggs directly in the oven
and praying they turn themselves into something

00:07:21.160 --> 00:07:23.130
that’s not total shit.

00:07:23.130 --> 00:07:28.530
Because Inflation Theory neatly answers so
many ‘well that’s not quite right’ dilemmas

00:07:28.530 --> 00:07:34.979
that exist in astrophysics, it’s since been
incorporated into the Big Bang Theory proper.When

00:07:34.979 --> 00:07:40.010
you plug inflation into a chalkboard covered
in the kind of pretty gibberish sometimes

00:07:40.010 --> 00:07:46.630
known as a scientific model, to say the output
is ‘interesting’ would be the biggest

00:07:46.630 --> 00:07:49.970
understatement in the history of mankind.

00:07:49.970 --> 00:07:55.690
Because almost all inflationary models of
the universe result in the existence of a

00:07:55.690 --> 00:07:57.240
multiverse.

00:07:57.240 --> 00:08:03.770
Take the Eternal Inflation model, for example,
which suggests that inflation is, you guessed

00:08:03.770 --> 00:08:05.639
it, eternal.

00:08:05.639 --> 00:08:11.039
According to the Eternal Inflation model,
as space inflates, increasing in size at an

00:08:11.039 --> 00:08:18.300
exponential rate, it creates many big bangs,
each of which results in a brand-new universe,

00:08:18.300 --> 00:08:19.710
just like our own.

00:08:19.710 --> 00:08:27.169
Our universe, therefore, is one of many so-called
‘pocket’ or ‘bubble’ universes within

00:08:27.169 --> 00:08:32.890
the eternal inflation of the parent universe,
whatever the bloody hell that is..

00:08:32.890 --> 00:08:40.130
It’s a very reasonable inference that if
inflation was the precursor to our own universe,

00:08:40.130 --> 00:08:43.560
then it could be to countless others too.

00:08:43.560 --> 00:08:51.200
And if inflation really is eternal there must
be an infinite number of pocket universes,

00:08:51.200 --> 00:08:54.220
too - one of which is ours.

00:08:54.220 --> 00:08:58.020
But this creates more questions than it answers,
such as:

00:08:58.020 --> 00:09:03.410
If there are infinite universes, how come
we find ourselves in this one?

00:09:03.410 --> 00:09:07.910
Do all the other universes contain life, or
only some?

00:09:07.910 --> 00:09:12.120
Do the other universes obey the same fundamental
laws as ours?

00:09:12.120 --> 00:09:14.930
And, which one is Star Trek set in?

00:09:14.930 --> 00:09:20.630
Those are some big questions, so how about
we scale things down a bit by substituting

00:09:20.630 --> 00:09:24.950
the word ‘universe’ with ‘planet’
in each of them.

00:09:24.950 --> 00:09:30.730
Luckily, science has been pondering the planetary
varieties of these posers ever since ancient

00:09:30.730 --> 00:09:36.540
astronomers first realised a few of those
pinpricks of light in the night sky weren’t

00:09:36.540 --> 00:09:39.110
stars at all, but nearby planets.

00:09:39.110 --> 00:09:48.569
And now here we are, in 2020, with 4,354 exoplanets
- that is, planets outside our own solar system

00:09:48.569 --> 00:09:53.589
- identified in our big book of things we
found lying around space.

00:09:53.589 --> 00:09:59.910
If our home is not as unique as once thought,
why do we live on this planet and not any

00:09:59.910 --> 00:10:05.480
one of an estimated 50 sextillion in the observable
universe?

00:10:05.480 --> 00:10:12.390
Well, scientists do have a fairly robust answer
to that - the goldilocks zone, also known

00:10:12.390 --> 00:10:17.120
as the habitable zone, which is the range
of orbits in which a planet is the perfect

00:10:17.120 --> 00:10:24.610
distance from its host star for surface temperatures
to support liquid water and, therefore, life.

00:10:24.610 --> 00:10:34.290
But a planet worthy of biological stirrings
needs other attributes besides just God-damn-impossibly-lucky-positioning.

00:10:34.290 --> 00:10:39.830
Such as a magnetic field to protect organisms
from deadly ionising cosmic radiation and

00:10:39.830 --> 00:10:42.620
a healthy dose of nitrogen in the atmosphere.

00:10:42.620 --> 00:10:48.330
The planet’s mass must fall within a very
specific range, too, so cell division is not

00:10:48.330 --> 00:10:54.640
impeded by its gravitational pull, or allowed
to just float around bumping into shit.

00:10:54.640 --> 00:11:02.610
Put simply, unless a planet is dealt pocket
aces, every hand, it can’t support life.

00:11:02.610 --> 00:11:08.959
Luckily for us, it just so happens our own
lovely planet had aces for days.

00:11:08.959 --> 00:11:13.709
If that all sounds kind of random, that’s
because it is.

00:11:13.709 --> 00:11:20.240
We only exist on this planet, in this quiet
corner of space, because we can exist here,

00:11:20.240 --> 00:11:22.430
so we do.

00:11:22.430 --> 00:11:27.690
We weren’t designed for our environment,
our environment designed us.

00:11:27.690 --> 00:11:34.399
We didn’t get lucky, we’re just a byproduct
of our planet arbitrarily ending up just where

00:11:34.399 --> 00:11:38.029
it needed to be in order to support life.

00:11:38.029 --> 00:11:43.310
Turns out, it’s kind of the same deal when
it comes to multiple universes.

00:11:43.310 --> 00:11:48.839
It’s likely that each universe would have
its own laws of physics, and could therefore

00:11:48.839 --> 00:11:53.470
potentially look and behave nothing like our
own.

00:11:53.470 --> 00:11:59.570
Other universes could have more, or fewer,
dimensions than the three we observe.

00:11:59.570 --> 00:12:05.410
If there are indeed an infinite number of
universes, we may just happen to live in one

00:12:05.410 --> 00:12:13.120
of the infinitesimally small percentile of
contenders that can host life – a goldilocks

00:12:13.120 --> 00:12:18.550
universe, if you will – with just the right
laws of physics, the ideal temperature and

00:12:18.550 --> 00:12:24.140
concentration of background radiation, and
the perfect size and shape to allow for life

00:12:24.140 --> 00:12:25.200
to germinate.

00:12:25.200 --> 00:12:32.180
We don’t know what that percentage is, yet,
but for every universe like ours, there could

00:12:32.180 --> 00:12:38.860
be a billion others that are dead - just empty
voids, perhaps with a few rocks and the occasional

00:12:38.860 --> 00:12:45.390
Cthulhu-esque interdimensional being floating
around, looking in vain for someone to hang

00:12:45.390 --> 00:12:46.480
out with.

00:12:46.480 --> 00:12:53.920
It’s actually the near-perfect conditions
and physical laws of our own universe which

00:12:53.920 --> 00:13:01.010
in many ways give credence to and a need for
the multiverse theory.

00:13:01.010 --> 00:13:06.330
If this was the one and only universe, it
would be awfully darn convenient that it just

00:13:06.330 --> 00:13:13.620
so happened to pop into existence 13.8 billion
years ago with the ideal conditions to support

00:13:13.620 --> 00:13:18.220
planets and lifeforms, on its very first attempt.

00:13:18.220 --> 00:13:24.910
If the universal strength of gravity varied
by just 1%, life as we know it could not exist

00:13:24.910 --> 00:13:26.660
in our universe.

00:13:26.660 --> 00:13:32.970
The same is true for any number of other parameters,
from the cosmological constant to the efficiency

00:13:32.970 --> 00:13:39.220
of fusion from hydrogen to helium and even
the number of dimensions, all of which had

00:13:39.220 --> 00:13:43.649
to be just so for us humans to exist.

00:13:43.649 --> 00:13:49.340
If, on the other hand, our universe is just
one of many, each with varying conditions,

00:13:49.340 --> 00:13:54.740
it’s rather less remarkable that we bagged
ourselves a goldilocks.

00:13:54.740 --> 00:14:01.139
It stands to reason that if we were to exist
at all we would do so in one of the potentially

00:14:01.139 --> 00:14:07.399
infinite universes with conditions perfect
for our kind of life, since we couldn’t

00:14:07.399 --> 00:14:09.930
have existed in any of the others.

00:14:09.930 --> 00:14:15.899
So it seems ‘multiverse’ is a neat way
for theoretical physicists to answer their

00:14:15.899 --> 00:14:17.079
unanswerables.

00:14:17.079 --> 00:14:19.480
Why is our universe like this?

00:14:19.480 --> 00:14:20.480
Multiverse.

00:14:20.480 --> 00:14:22.389
Why does life exist?

00:14:22.389 --> 00:14:23.389
Multiverse.

00:14:23.389 --> 00:14:25.540
Why does Donald Trump exist?

00:14:25.540 --> 00:14:26.600
Multiverse.

00:14:26.600 --> 00:14:31.870
But could we ever empirically prove our universe
is just one of many?

00:14:31.870 --> 00:14:37.150
Does this Truman Show have an outer wall,
perhaps even an exit?

00:14:37.150 --> 00:14:44.709
Or are we an eternally-lonely snowman trapped
in a snow globe – suspicious of another

00:14:44.709 --> 00:14:47.550
existence, but never quite sure?

00:14:47.550 --> 00:14:54.769
Well, like the snowman, we may only be able
to confirm other planes of existence if our

00:14:54.769 --> 00:14:56.339
own is destroyed.

00:14:56.339 --> 00:14:59.600
Or, at the very least, if it receives a good
wallop.

00:14:59.600 --> 00:15:07.200
It’s very possible that only if our bubble
universe collides with another will we, post-impact,

00:15:07.200 --> 00:15:13.269
be able to measure the effects of the collison
and definitively prove we have other universe

00:15:13.269 --> 00:15:15.110
pals out there.

00:15:15.110 --> 00:15:19.660
As you can imagine an entire fucking universe
smashing into another would be sort of a big

00:15:19.660 --> 00:15:20.660
deal.

00:15:20.660 --> 00:15:24.930
Depending on the velocity of the collision
– or the strength of other external forces

00:15:24.930 --> 00:15:31.579
that don’t even exist in our universe – it
could spell the end of both universes or merely

00:15:31.579 --> 00:15:33.540
leave a flesh wound.

00:15:33.540 --> 00:15:39.610
It really would be the cosmic equivalent of
a car crash, either a galactic bumper scrape

00:15:39.610 --> 00:15:42.540
or a multi-dimensional write-off.

00:15:42.540 --> 00:15:47.790
If it’s the latter, and we’re still here
afterwards wondering why the stars look a

00:15:47.790 --> 00:15:54.769
bit wobbly, we could measure the inevitable
results in the form of heat, radiation and

00:15:54.769 --> 00:15:56.850
other tell-tale signs.

00:15:56.850 --> 00:16:03.089
But it just so happens, we’ve already stumbled
across something a bit odd whilst poking around

00:16:03.089 --> 00:16:04.920
our cosmic backyard.

00:16:04.920 --> 00:16:13.720
In early 2000 the recently-launched Wilkinson
Microwave Anisotropy Probe, was – well – probing,

00:16:13.720 --> 00:16:16.680
when its data revealed something odd.

00:16:16.680 --> 00:16:27.970
A huge region of space within the constellation
Eridanus that’s unusually cold, a full 0.00007

00:16:27.970 --> 00:16:33.230
Kelvin colder than the average temperature
of the cosmic microwave background radiation

00:16:33.230 --> 00:16:35.900
– which is 2.7 Kelvin.

00:16:35.900 --> 00:16:38.530
OK, that doesn’t sound all that much.

00:16:38.530 --> 00:16:46.290
If my eyes were 0.00007 millimetres further
apart than the average, I wouldn’t be branded

00:16:46.290 --> 00:16:48.529
a mutant and publicly flogged.

00:16:48.529 --> 00:16:54.730
But remember, earlier I said our universe
is remarkably homogeneous.

00:16:54.730 --> 00:17:00.529
Compared to pretty much all the other nooks
and crannies we’ve probed... in space - get

00:17:00.529 --> 00:17:06.549
your mind out of the gutter! - – this particular
region is abnormally cold.

00:17:06.549 --> 00:17:10.639
On closer inspection, we found out why.

00:17:10.639 --> 00:17:17.529
Matter emits heat, and there’s 20% less
matter in this particular galactic neighbourhood

00:17:17.529 --> 00:17:19.169
than pretty much everywhere else.

00:17:19.169 --> 00:17:23.649
And this isn’t some pokey, out of the way
corner that can be explained away through

00:17:23.649 --> 00:17:25.899
some other anomaly.

00:17:25.899 --> 00:17:27.409
This thing is vast.

00:17:27.409 --> 00:17:35.049
At 1.8 billion light-years across, the aptly
named ‘Cold Spot’ is a relative cosmic

00:17:35.049 --> 00:17:36.049
desert.

00:17:36.049 --> 00:17:42.889
As yet, we have absolutely no idea what’s
actually there, but whatever it is, the cold

00:17:42.889 --> 00:17:49.850
spot is the largest contiguous structure we’ve
ever found in the observable universe.

00:17:49.850 --> 00:17:55.510
There are other cold regions of space - well
OK, all regions are space of cold, but you

00:17:55.510 --> 00:18:01.220
know what I mean - ‘colder’ regions of
space - but according to all popular scientific

00:18:01.220 --> 00:18:08.019
models of the universe, the existence of one
as large as this should be near impossible.

00:18:08.019 --> 00:18:15.309
Yet it very much does exist, raising the obvious
question, why?

00:18:15.309 --> 00:18:23.019
Well, a commonly espoused hypothesis is that,
at some time or other, another bubble universe

00:18:23.019 --> 00:18:24.710
collided with our own.

00:18:24.710 --> 00:18:30.600
When this happened matter was pushed away
from the impact site, leaving an imprint on

00:18:30.600 --> 00:18:37.179
our universe – a ‘space bruise’ if you
will – the cold spot.

00:18:37.179 --> 00:18:40.159
Colder spot.

00:18:40.159 --> 00:18:47.210
Despite eternal inflation, the cold spot,
and a few other clues, the existence of the

00:18:47.210 --> 00:18:54.470
multiverse remains no more than an idea tossed
around university chalkboards by a few cult-like

00:18:54.470 --> 00:18:55.470
believers.

00:18:55.470 --> 00:19:00.969
It’s viewed with scepticism by the scientific
masses and it’s not even a standalone hypothesis

00:19:00.969 --> 00:19:07.889
- the multiverse is the inevitable outcome
of more complete ideas, such as eternal inflation

00:19:07.889 --> 00:19:09.509
and string theory.

00:19:09.509 --> 00:19:14.830
We still have no way of testing models that
directly predict a multiverse, and until we

00:19:14.830 --> 00:19:22.009
do it will be relegated to a kind of grand
thought experiment and byproduct of sturdier

00:19:22.009 --> 00:19:23.479
models.

00:19:23.479 --> 00:19:27.879
Even the word itself is an oxymoron, when
you think about it.

00:19:27.879 --> 00:19:35.200
‘Universe’ means ‘everything’, so
by implication ‘multiverse’ means ‘multiple

00:19:35.200 --> 00:19:40.059
everythings’ – I’ll let you think about
that for a moment…

00:19:40.059 --> 00:19:44.860
Of course, this is no more than a linguistic
trap, and not quite grounds to call up Mr.

00:19:44.860 --> 00:19:49.610
Guth and tell him he’s a silly little muffin
who doesn’t know what he’s talking about.

00:19:49.610 --> 00:19:57.549
And if it does turn out to be true the implications
of a multiverse are equally incredible and

00:19:57.549 --> 00:19:59.440
terrifying.

00:19:59.440 --> 00:20:06.419
Infinite universes would mean there is a universe
identical to our own, with another you, and

00:20:06.419 --> 00:20:12.190
that there’s a universe where squirrels
are our despotic overlords and force us to

00:20:12.190 --> 00:20:15.649
farm acorns twenty hours a day.

00:20:15.649 --> 00:20:23.580
And, believe it or not, there’s even a universe
where the Star Wars reboots weren’t total

00:20:23.580 --> 00:20:26.730
shite.

00:20:26.730 --> 00:20:36.169
Thanks for watching.

